    "q-113227": {
      "dom_id": "step-q113227",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "What are the solutions of the equation $27{y}^{2}+63=0?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}27{y}^{2}+63 & =0 \\\\ 27{y}^{2} & =-63 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-\\frac{63}{27} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-\\frac{7}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy taking square root, we obtain:\n\n$$\ny=\\pm \\sqrt{-\\frac{7}{3}}\n$$\n\nWe now rewrite $y$ using the imaginary unit $\\text{i}=\\sqrt{-1}\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\pm \\sqrt{-\\frac{7}{3}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{7}{3}\\cdot (-1)} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{7}{3}}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{3}}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{3}}\\text{i} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{3}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
